    id: 'a2-adab-08', title: 'The Voice Note I Recorded Twice', level: 'A2', themeId: 'adab',
    text: `On Monday, a supplier sent the wrong materials to our office for the second time. I was responsible for receiving them, and I felt very annoyed.|I opened my phone and recorded a voice note to the supplier. My words were not insulting, but my tone sounded sharp and impatient.|Before sending it, I listened once. I imagined hearing that voice while trying to correct an honest mistake.|I deleted the recording and checked the order document. Then I discovered that one number on our own form was unclear.|The supplier still should have confirmed it, but the problem was not completely one-sided. I recorded a second message.|This time, I greeted him, described what had arrived, and sent a photo beside the written order. I asked how we could replace the materials before Thursday.|I also admitted that our form might have caused confusion. The message took only thirty seconds longer than the first version.|He called ten minutes later. He apologized, arranged a replacement, and asked us to type future item numbers instead of writing them by hand.|We agreed. The solution was simple once neither person was busy protecting pride.|That experience taught me to review more than the facts before sending a message. Tone also carries meaning, especially when people cannot see our face.|Politeness does not require hiding a real problem. My second note was still clear about the delay and the result we needed.|The difference was that it invited cooperation instead of beginning a fight.|Now, when I record a difficult message, I listen before sending. If I sound as if I am attacking the person, I pause and try again.|Good adab can live inside a voice note: a greeting, accurate details, fair responsibility, and words that leave room for a solution.`,
