    text: `My colleague invited several families to his home for dinner. I was glad to attend, but I had an early medical appointment the next morning.|I planned to leave at nine. When the time came, everyone was beginning another conversation, and the host was serving tea.|I did not want to interrupt the whole room with a long goodbye. I also did not want to disappear without thanking the host.|I waited until he finished serving, then spoke to him quietly near the kitchen. I thanked him for the meal and explained that I needed to leave early.|He understood immediately. He offered to pack dessert, but I declined because I had already eaten enough.|Before leaving, I greeted the older guests nearby. I did not walk around the entire house and stop every conversation.|The next day, I sent one short message saying I had enjoyed the evening and reached home safely.|Later, another guest told me that my exit had been almost invisible. At first, I wondered whether that was negative.|Then I realized a good departure does not need to become a second event. It should respect the host without taking attention from the gathering.|There are times when a guest must explain more, especially if leaving suddenly because of an emergency.|My situation was simple. A quiet word, sincere thanks, and no dramatic excuse were enough.|I also learned to mention time limits when accepting an invitation. It helps the host understand our schedule before the event begins.|At the next gathering, I told the host in advance that I could stay for one hour. There was no awkward moment when I left.|Good manners include knowing how to arrive and how to leave: warmly, honestly, and without making every movement about ourselves.`,
