    text: `Safa, may I share an observation about your presentation, or would you rather rest first?|Thank you for asking. I am still nervous, but I would like to hear one useful point.|Your examples were clear. I noticed that the final slide had much more text than the others.|I felt that too. I was afraid I would forget the conclusion, so I put every sentence there.|Would it help to keep three key phrases on the slide and place the full sentences in your private notes?|Yes. Then the audience could listen instead of trying to read a paragraph.|That was my thought. Do you want help choosing the three phrases?|Please. I appreciate that you started with what worked instead of saying the whole ending was bad.|I genuinely liked the examples. Feedback should be accurate, not sweet words followed by an attack.|Sometimes people say, “Can I be honest?” and then speak without care.|Honesty needs purpose and good timing. If advice only releases my frustration, it may not help you.|I also have feedback about your introduction from yesterday. Is now a good time?|Yes, it is. Please tell me.|The opening question was strong, but you answered it too quickly. A short pause would let listeners think.|That is specific and easy to practice. I will add a pause mark to my notes.|It feels easier to exchange advice when both people can accept, delay, or limit the conversation.|Exactly. Asking permission does not make feedback weak. It helps the other person become ready to use it.|Let us revise both sections, then practice once without stopping.|Good plan. Afterward, we can each name one improvement and finish for today.|Agreed. Useful advice should leave us clearer, not smaller.`,
