    text: `I stopped at a pharmacy after a long day because my mother needed her regular medicine. Only one pharmacist was working, and the queue barely moved.|A man near the front asked many questions. He opened several papers and called someone on his phone.|People behind me began sighing. One woman said loudly that some customers should prepare before reaching the counter.|I agreed inside my head. I looked at the time and imagined missing the bus home.|Then I heard part of the conversation. The man was collecting medicine for his father after a hospital visit and did not understand the new instructions.|The pharmacist was checking two medicines that should not be used together. The delay was protecting someone from a serious mistake.|My impatience changed into concern. I still wanted the line to move, but I no longer treated the man as a problem.|When he finished, he apologized to the queue. The woman who had complained looked away.|At my turn, the pharmacist also took time to confirm my mother's dose. I realized I wanted careful service for my family while demanding speed for everyone else.|Patience did not shorten the wait. It changed how I behaved during it.|I stopped making unhappy sounds, gave the person ahead enough space, and sent my mother a calm update.|The bus left before I reached the stop. I took the next one and arrived twenty minutes later than planned.|Nothing terrible happened. My earlier anger had been much larger than the real result.|Now I try to remember that a slow counter may contain a problem I cannot see.|Good character in a queue means respecting order, controlling harmful comments, and allowing workers to do careful work.`,
