    text: `Hasan, I told the community center I could design all twelve posters by Friday.|That is a large task. How many have you finished?|Only two. My work deadline also moved forward, and I am starting to panic.|Why did you agree to twelve?|They needed an answer during the meeting. Everyone looked at me, and I wanted to be helpful.|Wanting to help is good, but a promise should match your real ability.|I know. Should I stay awake for two nights and try to finish?|That may harm your work and the quality of the posters. Contact the organizer before the delay becomes a surprise.|I feel embarrassed because they trusted me.|You can admit that your estimate was wrong and bring a solution, not only a problem.|What solution would be fair?|Offer four finished posters by Friday, simple templates for the remaining events, and a schedule for completing them next week.|Could we also ask whether another volunteer can adapt the templates?|Yes, if the organizer agrees. Give clear files and explain the fonts and sizes.|I should not make a new promise before checking my calendar.|Exactly. Open it now and choose dates you can truly protect.|I can finish four by Friday and four on each of the next two Tuesdays.|Then say that clearly and accept if they need a different arrangement.|I will call this afternoon instead of sending a vague late-night message.|Good. Keeping trust after a poor promise begins with early honesty and a realistic repair plan.`,
