    text: `I believed I understood my daily spending because I rarely bought expensive things. Still, my account balance always surprised me near the end of the month.|For one week, I wrote down every payment immediately. I used a small note on my phone with only four categories.|The first category was meals, the second was transport, the third was household needs, and the fourth was optional spending.|On Monday, the list looked ordinary. By Wednesday, I noticed that I had bought drinks and snacks six times.|None of them cost much, but together they were almost equal to two full meals.|I also paid for short motorcycle rides when I could have walked safely in fifteen minutes. I used them mostly because I left home late.|The record did not tell me to remove every comfort. It showed which choices were automatic rather than useful.|For the rest of the week, I carried water and one snack. I also prepared my bag earlier, so I could walk to nearby places without rushing.|On Sunday, I reviewed the list. Household spending was reasonable, and I did not feel guilty about lunch with a friend.|The biggest opportunity was not the social meal. It was the repeated spending I barely remembered.|I created a simple monthly amount for snacks and transport. When I choose to spend it, I want the choice to be visible.|I also kept a small amount for generosity and unexpected needs, because a budget should not make a person hard-hearted or unable to help.|Writing everything forever may be tiring, so now I track closely for one week each month.|The exercise gives me an honest picture without turning every coin into anxiety.|A useful budget is not punishment. It is information that helps money follow our real priorities.`,
