    text: `I borrowed a popular history book because several people online called it essential. The first chapter was interesting, but the language soon became very difficult.|I read the same page three times and understood only the general topic. Still, I carried the book for two weeks because returning it felt like failure.|At the library, I finally asked a librarian for help. She did not tell me to try harder or choose an easier hobby.|She asked what part of the subject interested me most. I said I wanted to understand how ordinary families lived during that period.|The librarian brought three alternatives: a shorter overview, a collection of letters with explanations, and an illustrated timeline.|She explained that a difficult book may be excellent but wrong for a reader's current purpose or background knowledge.|I chose the overview and the letters. The overview gave me names and dates, while the letters made daily life feel real.|After finishing both, I returned to one chapter of the original book. This time, several references made sense.|I still did not read the whole book. Instead, I used its index to find sections connected to my questions.|That approach changed my idea of serious reading. Finishing every page is not the only sign of learning.|Choosing an entry point, building background, and reading selected sections can be more honest than pretending to understand.|I also stopped treating popularity as a personal command. A book can be valuable without being the right next book for me.|The librarian helped me create a path instead of handing me a simple replacement.|Now, before borrowing a challenging book, I ask what I want from it and what knowledge it expects.|Leaving one book unfinished led me to read two others carefully and return with better questions.`,
