    speakers: [{ name: 'Nisa', gender: 'female' }, { name: 'Mr. Adnan', gender: 'male' }],
    text: `Mr. Adnan, my teaching interview includes a five-minute lesson. I have planned twelve slides.|Twelve slides in five minutes may leave no time for learners to think. What is your single learning goal?|I want them to identify three signs of a reliable online source.|That is clear. Can they practice the skill during the lesson?|I planned to explain all three signs and show many examples.|Choose one short example first. Ask learners what they notice before you explain.|So the first minute could show two websites with different authors and dates.|Yes. Then collect two observations, introduce the three signs, and let them check one new example.|I am worried silence will make me look unprepared.|A brief thinking pause shows that you expect learners to participate. Tell them they have ten seconds.|How should I end the demo?|Ask one final question that reveals whether they can use the idea, not whether they remember your definition.|Perhaps: “Which source would you open first, and what would you check?”|Good. Their reason matters more than choosing the picture you prefer.|Do I need printed materials?|One half-page example is enough. Bring a backup in case the screen does not work.|I will reduce the slides to four: goal, comparison, three signs, and final task.|Practice with a timer, including the pause and one possible student answer.|What if the interviewer gives no answer?|Say what a learner might notice and continue calmly. Do not turn the demo into a rushed speech.|I understand. Five minutes should show one complete learning moment, not a whole course.`,
